<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cen Chunxuan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1861">1861</a>–<a href="/years/1933">1933</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cen Chunxuan was a late Qing official who served as governor-general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> and of Liangguang. In the early Republic he opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai">Yuan Shikai</a>, and from 1918 he chaired the directorate of the military government at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a> generals forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> out. His government collapsed in 1920, and he retired from politics.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cen-chunxuan.jpg" alt="Cen Chunxuan" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B2%91%E6%98%A5%E7%85%8A.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cen was born on 2 May 1861 in Xilin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a>, a Zhuang Chinese and the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cen-yuying">Cen Yuying</a>, Viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guizhou">Guizhou</a>. Badly behaved in his youth, he was counted among the Three Notorious Youngsters in the Capital. He entered the civil service in 1879 and passed the imperial examination as a juren in 1885. In 1898 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxu-emperor">Guangxu Emperor</a>, impressed after interviewing him, made an exception and promoted him to a second-grade post in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>, but a quarrel with his superior, Tan Zhonglin, had him moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When the Eight-Nation Alliance attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in 1900, Cen led troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> to protect the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxu-emperor">Guangxu Emperor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Empress Dowager Cixi</a> on their flight to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a>, winning her favour. As governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a> he set up the forerunner of Shanxi University with the Welsh missionary Timothy Richard. As acting viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> he set up a police force and accused more than 40 officials of corruption, earning the name Butcher of Officials. Appointed Viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a> in 1903, he urged a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> and the abolition of the imperial examinations, and became a leader of the Constitutional Monarchy Movement.</p><p>His attacks on corrupt officials made an enemy of Prince Qing, who with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai">Yuan Shikai</a> defeated him in the political struggle of dingwei: transferred to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> in 1906, briefly Minister of Posts and Communications in 1907, he was accused of supporting Liang Qichao and dismissed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Cixi</a>. In 1911 he urged the court to become a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> rather than crush the rebels, and resigned shortly before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuchang-uprising">Wuchang Uprising</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Under the Republic he asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai">Yuan Shikai</a> in 1913 to settle his conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> peacefully; the revolutionaries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-revolution">Second Revolution</a> named him their grand marshal, and after its failure he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> with a price on his head. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> on 4 January 1916 to oppose Yuan's attempt to make himself emperor, and on 19 April 1916 he, Liang Qichao and Lu Rongting set up the National Protection Military Government at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhaoqing">Zhaoqing</a>. When the military government he led at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a> from 1918 was disbanded two years later, he resigned and lived in retirement in Shanghai, where he died on 27 April 1933.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cen Chunxuan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The government depended on Guangxi and Yunnan generals, who forced Sun out in 1918 and ruled through a directorate under <b>Cen Chunxuan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/canton-military-government">Canton Military Government</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Xilin County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=24.49,105.09&amp;year=1907" aria-label="Xilin County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Cen Chunxuan"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3853 5395h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3853 5395h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=24.49,105.09&amp;year=1907">What was here in 1907? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
